# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) for local dev, and set the same variable in the
# GitHub Actions deploy workflow for production.
#
# Google OAuth client id for free-tier backup to the user's OWN Google Drive.
# Without it, lib/drive.ts is dormant: the Backup section never renders, no
# third-party script is loaded, and the app behaves exactly as it did before the
# feature existed. Nothing else in the app reads it.
#
# Create at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   · Application type: Web application
#   · Authorised JavaScript origins: your dev origin + https://corruptfun.github.io
#   · Scope requested at runtime: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.appdata
#     (app-private folder only — never the user's files, which is what keeps
#     this out of Google's restricted-scope review)
#
# There is NO client secret. This is a browser token flow; the id is public and
# ships in the bundle, which is expected and safe for this OAuth model.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# PSA public-API token, used to resolve a scanned slab's certification number
# to the exact card (lib/psa.ts). This is OUR key — there is no user-facing
# field for it — so cert lookup works with nothing to configure. Leave it empty
# and the module is dormant: no request is ever made to PSA, and slab scanning
# still works off the printed label.
#
# Create at https://www.psacard.com/publicapi (free; the token does not expire).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT. Unlike VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above, this value is
# NOT safe to publish. That client id is public by design and protected by its
# origin allowlist; a PSA bearer token has no such backstop and ships readable
# inside a static bundle. Two things follow:
#
#   1. Anyone can extract it and spend it against our PSA account.
#   2. The free tier is ~100 calls a DAY for the whole account, now shared by
#      every user rather than per-person. Ordinary traffic exhausts it.
#
# Certs are cached for months and a 429 stands lookups down for hours, but the
# arithmetic is unchanged. The real fix is a proxy holding the token
# server-side with per-user rate limiting — point VITE_PSA_ENDPOINT at it and
# leave this empty.
VITE_PSA_TOKEN=

# Optional: base URL of a cert-lookup proxy, called as `<endpoint>/<cert>`.
# Overrides PSA's own endpoint so the token never has to reach the browser.
VITE_PSA_ENDPOINT=
